PREFACE
Game changing. Breakthrough. Revolutionary. Disruptive. Transformative. These are the types of terms that are being used to describe additive manufacturing (AM). Metal AM has been a primary focus of advanced manufacturing in the aerospace industry since it became mainstream in the early 2010s. AM related advances in new industries, supply chains, broadened design opportunities, and metals and metal alloys are increasing at a rapid pace. The goal of this book is to provide an overview of the concept-to-utilization life cycle in AM for propulsion applications. As with any new process, it is tempting to become captivated by the excitement of AM and its application and to give little forethought to the potential economic and technical challenges. It is our hope that this book will help AM adopters to be intentional as they apply these technologies and to understand the basis for the life cycle of AM components. We want to provide a practical guide based on lessons learned and experiences with both successes and failures in AM. The organization of this book seeks to guide the engineer through the intertwined basics, regarding design and implementation, as they begin their journey into AM. This guide is also relevant for veteran AM users as a reference and introduction to emerging novel AM methods. Although AM in many ways is still in the development phase and continues to evolve, the fundamental lessons learned remain of great value even as forthcoming technology emerges. Although we recognize and reference many metal AM processes, we focus herein on those with more industry maturity, including laser powder bed fusion and laser powder directed energy deposition. Although the structure of this book forces topics into a logical and sequential manner, in practice, the life-cycle process of AM for a design application rarely flows sequentially. The order of several chapters and sections were the topic of much spirited debate among the editors. The result is chapter subsections with wide-ranging relevance both across chapters and within individual chapters. In fact, this intertwined nature is one of the AM lessons that we wish to impress upon the reader. The proper implementation of AM requires a thorough understanding of the entire life-cycle process. (Please re-read that sentence.) An engineer will not be able to take full advantage of AM unless they have an understanding of the metals, processes, post-processing, performance of AM parts, and approach for implementation and certification. The roles of design engineer, materials engineer, and manufacturing engineer are more closely related when AM is employed, and these multi-disciplinary roles are intertwined in the adoption of AM in the aerospace industry. The propulsion industry is adopting these AM processes but is also progressing through the challenges that come with implementing new technologies. New failure modes are being introduced and better understood as AM processes continue to evolve. AM is offering a new fourth industrial revolution and design
