solutions that were not previously possible. The hype often associated with AM can lead to rapid incorporation of AM technologies without regard for the challenges of sustained implementation. This leads to another of our goals for this book, which is to provide advocacy for the technology while simultaneously delivering a realistic overview of the design and process life cycle. This approach allows strategic AM development through certification, approached with a fundamental understanding so that application of the technology can remain directed at proper implementation. To that end, trade studies and examples of appropriate and inappropriate applications of AM technologies are included. One final note regarding the terminology associated with AM, more commonly known as 3D printing. Throughout this book, you will find reference to the technology as AM or additive manufacturing. Why? This is because additive manufacturing is the ASTM standard terminology (ISO/ASTM52900) adopted by the technical community. 3D printing is a generalized term and usually references polymer printing. The focus of this book is on metal. To confuse this issue even more, you will find references to many other terms that all mean AM, such as additive, building, growing, melting, and yes, sometimes printing. Our attempt is to distill as much information as possible, including applications and technology fundamentals, and to allow for an interesting and informative read. Another point of nomenclature, throughout this book, metal alloys are referred to as metals, alloys, or metal alloys, and these terms are used interchangeably. We occasionally mention materials, such as material properties, but this also refers to metal in accordance with the book title. An overview of each of the chapters is provided to help guide the reader through the intertwined journey with metal AM for propulsion applications.
