1.1. INTRODUCTION
Additive manufacturing (AM) technologies have proven to be radically different from traditional approaches and are grabbing the attention of the propulsion industry. The decade of 2010 – 2020 was when metal AM took hold across a variety of industries. Large and small companies across the aerospace, automotive, energy, oil and gas, tool and die, and medical industries, as well as many others, are embracing the technology. The industrial age rapidly advanced manufacturing and modern society. The modern digital and information ages are transforming design and fabrication through AM, earning this period the moniker the Fourth Industrial Revolution [1, 2]. These transformations are changing the speed and performance of parts being fabricated. There is clear evidence behind the interest in metal AM, with aerospace components having lead times shortened by 70%, nonrecurring costs reduced by 50% or more, and masses decreased by 35% or more [3, 4]. New industries and companies are being created as AM reduces entry costs and opens design freedoms. New supply chains are evolving as AM is making fabrication accessible to companies and applications, including critical spares, for which it was previously difficult or cost-prohibitive [5]. Significant research in AM is being conducted across government, industry, and academia, and although this technology is rapidly evolving, it remains in its technological adolescence. The aerospace industry has been quick to adopt metal AM, and the focus of this book is specifically on propulsion systems. It is true that many of the metal alloys and the environments in which they operate are more extreme for aerospace than many other industries. Nevertheless, although the applications discussed may be specific to propulsion applications, many of the lessons learned can be adopted in a variety of other industries. Aerospace propulsion components include some of the most complex parts required across manufacturing industries. These components operate in some of the harshest environments known, they are required to have high reliability,
