1.1.1. ADVANTAGES OF METAL ADDITIVE MANUFACTURING
There are several advantages of AM that make it attractive for propulsion components. AM helps solve many of the complexities introduced during traditional manufacturing and assembly operations and can significantly reduce the lead time for fabrication, reduce overall life-cycle costs, and yield performance improvements resulting from new design freedoms. Such design freedom allows engineers to embrace more complex geometries in cases where complexity was desired but not manufacturable with traditional techniques. There is additional freedom for the engineer to simplify designs, such as by reducing part count in designs that previously required multiple parts to be joined. More specific to cost, schedule, and performance improvements, these characteristics of the AM process make it attractive for propulsion component applications:
1. No or limited tooling is required.
2. Reductions in part count or mass can be achieved through increased design freedom.
3. A broad range of metal alloys can be used.
4. Various sized and featured parts can be manufactured using different AM processes.
5. Overall processing time and subsequent cost are reduced.
6. Design freedom is increased, as fewer manufacturing constraints are imposed to enhance performance.
7. Production lead time is reduced.
8. New supply chains, such as critical spares, are enabled.
Based on these factors, the ideal applications for AM are parts and systems with complex features, unique metal alloys, or alloys that are difficult to process with traditional techniques, and low production rates, as well as those applications for which the AM process offers a significant advantage in terms of lead time (and consequently cost). This can be seen in the Venn diagram in Fig. 1.4. Within this context, AM provides other significant opportunities for performance enhancements, such as mass reductions, optimized fluid paths, or heat-transfer functionality. Many propulsion components tend to incorporate many or all of these aspects. Propulsion components have high complexity because they are optimized for mass and extreme environments. This drives the use of unique metal alloys that are often difficult to machine or form with traditional methods. Because of the complexity of the designs, propulsion components often have very long lead times, starting with the wrought metal through completion and
