1. High-complexity applications The complexity associated with many metal AM processes is higher than that available with traditional manufacturing processes. Consequently, part complexity that was previously unaffordable can now be specified within production systems. This complexity can be used to increase structural efficiency, thereby reducing system mass and increasing payload capacity or range, or it may allow for flow features or passages that are not possible with traditional methods. Furthermore, the material usage yield is high, with unmelted powder being available for recycling, thereby providing tangible economic advantages, especially for the high-cost metal alloys of interest to propulsion applications. The capacity for high system complexity can be used to enable the consolidation of parts, which can reduce costs by eliminating secondary processing, joining, and certification methods required for traditional manufacture. Additional complexity is being explored using generative design and topology optimization offering mass and thermal optimization when known loads and known material properties are available with certainty. Although the complexity offers advantages, the entire AM process should be considered in cost trade-offs, as the increased complexity may drive high costs for inspections or, in some cases, challenging certification approaches.
