1.1.2. CHALLENGES WITH METAL ADDITIVE MANUFACTURING
Although the advantages of AM are numerous, AM is not always the best manufacturing solution for every component. Rather, AM is another manufacturing process available that must be properly traded against traditional manufacturing and other advanced manufacturing processes during the design stage of the product life cycle. Unique rationale, design attributes, metal alloy needs, process availability, or economics should drive the decision toward AM. There are also additional considerations when trading the AM processes.
1. Production volume and time can be limited.
2. Many metal alloys can be used, but they typically must be weldable and still require a powder or raw material supply chain.
3. Distortion and residual stresses are intrinsic to the melt and solidification AM processes.
4. The entire AM process from design to service application must be wellunderstood and considered.
5. Variations occur across different processes.
6. Not all AM machine platforms can build the same part.
7. AM machines represent a significant capital investment and can present a barrier to entry.
The production rate or volume of parts being fabricated with AM is going to be limited compared to high-volume traditional manufacturing techniques. Although technologies to improve productivity in AM technologies are under investigation, many additive systems, specifically in the PBF category, are currently limited in the available physical build volume. To increase production volume, additional machines must be added, and high production volume may not be feasible or may be lower in cost with traditional processes. As part of the initial design trade studies, the part life cycle and production rates should be thoroughly evaluated. Traditional manufacturing technologies may trade more positively as volumes increase significantly and complexity remains low. Additive manufacturing is best suited for lower-volume and high-complexity components, including designs that cannot be built otherwise. This is one of the prime opportunities where propulsion systems can take full advantage of AM as such systems often involve lower production rates. If parts are small enough, multiples can fit on a single build plate, offering increased production capabilities, but this advantage must be properly evaluated including postprocessing operations. The build time for AM is fixed. The time required to fabricate the initial development prototype compared to the time it takes to build the nth unit using AM is not going to radically change. Although some learning-curve advantages can be incorporated to reduce some build time from the first prototype, these advantages
