1.3. REDUCING ENTRY BARRIERS FOR ROCKET ENGINES
Metal AM has spawned several rocket propulsion and launch vehicle companies whose business models rely on the capabilities provided by AM. One of the prior barriers to entry was the complexity and time required to manufacture liquid rocket engine components such as the combustion chambers, injectors, turbomachinery, ducts, and valves. Organizations and companies now have the ability to fabricate fully functional parts that were once severely limited by cost, lead time and specialty skills. The reductions in cost and lead time through AM allow these companies to complete early testing and revise designs, reducing high-cost changes when a part has issues late in the development of an engine. Many aerospace companies and government organizations, such as NASA, led early development to prove feasibility to the community. NASA, along with prime contractor Aerojet Rocketdyne, started evaluating L-PBF technology in 2010 for a simple hot-gas exhaust duct for the development of the J-2X liquid-hydrogen (LH2)/liquid-oxygen (LOX) rocket engine [59]. Successful testing of this component demonstrated functional metal AM components in an extreme rocket engine environment. The traditional method of forming the 102 mm (4 in.) diameter Inconel 625 duct was by thick wall tube bending. Because of design changes required from component testing of the gas generator injector, which mated with the duct, a bend radius equivalent to the tube diameter was required for a shorter length. Fabrication of this new duct design was not feasible as a single piece using traditional methods. The tube would have had to have been formed in three pieces and welded together. The traditional approach also yielded thinning during the bending operations and created issues with weld offsets. As one would expect, this process was labor-intensive, expensive, and caused challenges in welding and inspection. L-PBF AM was offered as a potential solution, which required no tooling and allowed the design to be fabricated as a single piece using the same metal alloy. Although this duct is a simple example of AM in an aerospace application, it was shown to significantly reduce the schedule of a part that required the use of metal alloys that are otherwise difficult to process. The time from the initial evaluation of AM for this component to the fabrication of a manufacturing demonstrator unit was less than two months. The L-PBF process required no special tooling, which alone would have taken two months to fabricate (Fig. 1.15). Additionally, a uniform wall thickness could be achieved, providing an advantage over the tube bending approach. After AM of this duct, including nondestructive evaluations (NDEs) and inspections, surface finish post-processing, and proof testing, the duct was hot-fire tested in a dynamic hot-gas environment exceeding 4278C
