margins to reduce overall mass, thereby allowing for more payload on the vehicle. Regeneratively cooled chambers have a series of thin-wall cooling channels that maintain integrity of a very thin hot wall, or region between the coolant and the hot combustion gases. Each of these channels must contain high-pressure coolant, which is then generally supplied to the injector after cooling the chamber. Hydraulic performance and thermal performance within the channels are balanced to optimize the overall performance. All of these design requirements flow into the final design and ultimately make manufacturing very challenging. Traditional manufacturing process solutions for combustion chambers include a tube-wall brazed design, a channel-wall brazed design, and a channelwall electroplated design. All of these manufacturing processes require many piece parts and laborious machining or significant assembly and joining to be successful (Fig. 1.17). The more traditional methods of manufacturing a regeneratively or dump-cooled combustion chamber are the brazed or plated design. These designs started out as a cast and forged liner that was machined, had channels slotted into it, and was then closed out using an electroplating process or a brazing process [63, 64]. The general plating process can be seen in Fig. 1.17. This figure is highly simplified and does not show the extensive tooling, many additional processes such as waxing and removal, and other interim steps required throughout the processing. The brazing process is similar and requires a tighttolerance assembly, various tooling, a furnace brazing operation, and significant know-how to seal (close out) the slotted liner successfully without leaks. These manufacturing techniques described for combustion chambers were mostly developed prior to 1960 and often suffered a high reject rate. Compare this traditional design to an AM chamber process that combines many process steps into a single operation with “no assembly requ