2.1. INTRODUCTION
Additive manufacturing (AM) processes for metals and metal alloys have rapidly matured since the first processes were marketed in the late 1990s. To date, a wide variety of metal AM processes have been developed, and many are discussed in this chapter. However, two types of processes, powder bed fusion (PBF) and directed energy deposition (DED), are much more widely used than other processes. PBF is a process that allows for the fabrication of complex, highly detailed metal components. PBF systems use a powder feedstock that is spread onto a substrate by powder dispensing and recoating systems. The distributed powder feedstock is then melted layer by layer using a focused energy source, such as a laser or
