electron beam. Unlike PBF, DED allows for freeform fabrication rather than layer-by-layer fabrication in a powder bed. The process is accomplished by depositing either a blown powder or wire feedstock into a thermal-energy-sourcedirected deposition region on the part. The energy source is generally a laser but can also be an electron beam or electric arc. Machine vendors have continually refined and improved these processes over several decades, and the evolution of the technologies is discussed herein. A significant focus of this chapter is PBF and DED, specifically laser powder bed fusion (L-PBF) and laser powder directed energy deposition (LP-DED). Other metal AM processes are also discussed in this chapter that include various forms of DED, PBF and solid-state processes using ultrasonic, binder jetting, cold spray, and friction stir to deposit material. A major barrier to the adoption of DED and L-PBF in the early 2000s was a lack of the reliability and accuracy needed by the aerospace marketplace for the development of flight hardware [1]. Since then, the technology has evolved to the point that larger L-PBF builds can run for many consecutive weeks and, in some cases, have been demonstrated for several months in duration without build failure. This evolution can be credited to the development of solid-state laser and computational technology. Prior to the existence of solid-state lasers, build times were limited by the pumped laser technology at the time and the durations that could be maintained continuously and accurately. This time was much shorter than the weeks of continuous weld time typically needed to produce a part. Electron beam systems matured early on during this time as a potential answer because of their inherent high power levels and high levels of control, enabling large impacts in the medical device industry through the shaping of Ti6Al4V and CoCr alloys. Eventually, the new solid-state laser systems overcame many of the issues related to continuous operation, and they have proven to be more stable and precise than electron beam devices because of their smaller beam diameters. Another barrier to the evolution and therefore adoption of PBF and DED processes by industry was the lack of focus on material properties during initial research and development. In other words, early during the development of AM technologies, the focus of the engineers designing and building the systems was more on system functionality and less on material properties. Many of these AM laboratories were located in university mechanical engineering departments with the knowledge of controls, computer-aided design (CAD), and design expertise to make the systems work and melt feedstock to produce geometries, but with inconsistent properties. As the functionality of these systems improved and material scientists and engineers became more involved, material properties slowly became more consistent. This can be observed in the literature, as more characterization papers using optical microscopy, scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and microstructure of the processes started to be published. The issues with oxidation, solidification, lack of fusion (LOF), chemistry loss, gas flow, and internal stresses were studied more closely. As some of the issues were resolved, the
