properties and repeatability of the process began to improve, and the technology became more popular. Despite the existence of many issues that required additional resolution and maturation, L-PBF and DED processes were advanced by a few machine manufacturers. As an example, two major issues that the industry had to overcome early on were gas flow for efficient effluent removal and residual stress buildup created inherently by the layer-by-layer welding procedure. Additionally, limited options for machine vendors may have been one of the largest hurdles for more than a decade. Because of substantial cost reductions, patent expirations in 2010, and re-licensing of many laser and optics systems there is now a wide array of L-PBF machine vendors with increasingly better capabilities. Many of these systems incorporate both single and multiple lasers to increase build speeds, some with build volumes of up to 1 1 1 meter, thus rivaling DED build volumes. In addition to L-PBF and DED, many other metal AM systems have also evolved. Each process has various advantages and disadvantages that are application-specific and metal-alloy-specific. However, each process has a place in the AM space based on the component application. Descriptions of the following processes are provided in this chapter:
1. Laser powder bed fusion (L-PBF) fabricates parts using a bed of powder feedstock and a laser that melts the metal powder based on the defined scan strategy to create features within a discrete layer. The process chamber atmosphere is inert, flowing gas across the build continuously removes particulate ejecta, and a recoater arm provides a new layer of powder to be melted in a layer-by-layer operation, thereby building a final part.
2. Laser powder directed energy deposition (LP-DED) allows for the fabrication of parts by directing blown powder into a laser generated melt pool to create deposited beads. Based on the multi-axis motion control system, freeform parts are built. A local inert-gas purge or fabrication within an inert environment is typical.
3. Electron beam powder bed fusion (EB-PBF) is similar to L-PBF, but it uses an electron beam (EB) within a vacuum or rarefied inert atmosphere where the EB preheats the layer and then melts the metal based on the defined toolpath (layer-by-layer) to produce a final part.
4. Arc-wire directed energy deposition (AW-DED) uses an electric arc and wire feedstock actively fed into the melt pool to deposit beads with a local inert-gas purge. The deposition head is adapted to a robot or gantry system to provide freeform part structures.
5. Laser wire directed energy deposition (LW-DED) fabricates parts using a wire fed into a melt pool created by the laser to deposit beads. A multi-axis
