Downloaded by Michael Brooks on February 26, 2025 | http://arc.aiaa.org | DOI: 10.2514/4.106279


motion control system can be used to build freeform parts. A local purge or fabrication within an inert environment is typical.
6. Laser hot-wire directed energy deposition (LHW-DED) is similar to LW-DED, but with the addition of a secondary heat source that preheats the wire. A laser is used to create the melt pool, and the preheating allows for a lower-temperature melt pool. A local inert-gas purge or fabrication within an inert environment is typical.
7. Electron beam wire directed energy deposition (EBW-DED) uses an electron beam and an off-axis wire feed to deposit metal inside a vacuum chamber. The electron beam moves along the Z (height) axis, whereas the part is moved on a Cartesian coordinate system and trunnion table to create freeform structures.
8. Additive friction stir-deposition (AFS-D) is a solid-state AM process that uses rod or powder feedstock fed into a rotating friction stir pin tool. When downward force is applied, the metal experiences plastic deformation and is solid-state welded in a layer-by-layer fashion. A table beneath the deposition head provides the motion to create parts.
9. Ultrasonic additive manufacturing (UAM) uses a high-frequency transducer and foil or metal tape feedstock under high contact pressure to create solid-state metallurgical bonding of these thin layers. A standard computer numerical control (CNC) milling head is used between layers to subtract material and create the desired features.
10. Cold spray uses a converging–diverging supersonic nozzle that injects highpressure inert gas and metal powder feedstock, which is sprayed against a backing surface to form structures.
11. Binder jetting uses an inkjet printhead to loosely bind metal powder layer-by-layer to build a part that is later consolidated to its final density in a furnace to undergo binder burn-out and part sintering. The printing is conducted at room temperature and in air.
Some of these processes, namely, L-PBF, EB-PBF, and DED, have matured and are being used actively in propulsion flight applications. Other processes are still at a lower technology readiness level (TRL) and are being developed and tested in ground applications with limited flight experience. The purpose of this chapter is to familiarize the reader with each of the processes, provide various factors to trade in each of the processes, and introduce some considerations in process limitations that could affect design features and subsequent performance of the components. A detailed discussion is included on each of the metal AM processes, along with process-specific information that is relevant to the needs of design, manufacturing, and materials engineers. Although many AM processes exist and are maturing, the primary focus for propulsion
