PBF, the primary discriminator between processes is the energy source used to locally melt powder and form the component shape in a layer-by-layer process [7, 8]. The two PBF processes, L-PBF, which uses a laser energy source, and EB-PBF, which uses an electron beam energy source, both have very large supply chains available. These two processes using melted metal are described in further detail in this chapter. With regard to DED, there are many processes that use feedstock in the form of either powder or wire. DED can use various energy sources to melt the feedstock metal, including laser, electron beam, electric arc, and resistance arc. Each of the metal AM processes that melt material have advantages and disadvantages, as discussed further herein. A primary difference to consider among the melting processes is the method of heat input, which drives deposition rates, or the amount of metal that can be deposited within a time period. Deposition rate is known to be critical for determining both the cost and quality of metal AM parts because increasing time on the AM machine increases costs as well as residual stresses. The second discriminator is the feature resolution that can be built using the process, where PBF processes have higher resolution than DED processes. The third discriminator is overall build volume, with DED processes providing
