2.1.1 PROCESS SELECTION CRITERIA
Several discriminators must be considered when evaluating which metal AM process might be most appropriate for an application. It should be noted that many of these trade-offs are interrelated, which will become apparent to the reader in further discussion. These discriminators include the following: 1) industrial maturity of the process, 2) complexity of the part, 3) feature resolution, 4) scale of the part, 5) required properties and material physics, 6) speed of the process (closely related to cost), 7) availability of the process and supply chain, and 8) integrated materials using bimetallic or monolithic alloys. A detailed discussion of process selection for metal AM aerospace components is detailed by Gradl et al. [10]. Although the core technologies of metal AM processes have been around for decades, these technologies continue to be better integrated, further optimized, providing better repeatability and more complex features during fabrication. However, some processes are much more mature than others because of early industry adoption, development of standards for flight applications, and evolution and characterization of the materials used in the process. These factors help determine the TRL of the process [11]. The most common metal AM process in the aerospace industry is L-PBF. L-PBF was adopted rapidly for aerospace, specifically for use in propulsion applications, because of its ability to fabricate complex fine feature parts directly. Consequently, the L-PBF process is the most mature and has the highest TRL [12]. For example, industry adoption of the process led to NASA and the U.S. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) creating certification specifications to allow use of this process in flight hardware applications, as discussed further in Chapter 9 [13]. A main consideration of TRL and industrialization is the maturity of the database for material properties, design allowables, and classification of materials for the statistical basis of properties (e.g., A-basis, B-basis). The programmatic reasons for using AM in aerospace include cost and schedule savings; however, the major advantage of using AM in aerospace is the ability to make highly complex parts, including parts with features that were not previously possible with traditional techniques. In this case, complexity refers to small and/or intricate features, such as holes, walls, and internal passages, in addition to freeform three-dimensional surfaces. The degree of complexity of a part design must be strongly considered when selecting a metal AM process because each AM process has its own unique limits, including the complexity of internal and external features [14]. L-PBF offers advantages over other metal AM processes because it can achieve very fine feature resolution, including internal flow passages and near-final-shape highly complex surfaces. In fact, the details of fine holes or internal passages that
