2.1.2. AM COORDINATE SYSTEMS
It is important for users to understand build direction and orientation of parts, so this section provides an overview of AM coordinate systems. Coordinate systems for AM are standardized in ASTM 52921, which begins with ISO 841 as its base [51]. The basic machine coordinate system is a three-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system of X, Y, and Z for displacements and A, B, and C, respectively, for rotational directions about these axes. The origin of the system resides on the build plate inside the machine. The positive directions for the rotational axes follow the right-hand rule. For layer-based processes such as L-PBF (Fig. 2.8), the Z axis is taken as the direction of layer addition. The X axis is parallel to the front of the machine, with the front of the machine being designated by the machine manufacturer. It is usually the face of the machine with operator access and with the primary viewing window. The positive X direction is from left to right as viewed from the front of the machine. The positive Y axis is then determined by the right-hand rule. The result is that, for machines that print upward, such as L-PBF, positive Z is up, and the positive Y axis runs toward the back of the machine. For machines that add material downward, the result is that the positive Y axis runs toward the front of the machine.
