2.2. LASER POWDER BED FUSION (L-PBF)
2.2.1. INTRODUCTION
PBF is a general term used to describe AM processes that deposit a layer of powder feedstock onto a base metal and melt it using a high-power energy source, be it a laser (L-PBF) or electron beam (EB-PBF). L-PBF is one of the most mature industrial metal AM processes at the time as of 2021. L-PBF offers exceptional feature resolution and fine microstructure, and it enables use of a wide variety of metals and metal alloys. L-PBF is also commonly referred to by machine vendor trade names such as selective laser melting (SLM), direct metal laser sintering (DMLS), and direct metal laser melting (DMLM). L-PBF uses a laser to precisely melt thin layers of metal powder to form a solid cross section. This is done layer-by-layer until a full part has been built. It is useful to think of L-PBF as a microweld process, whereby each laser pass forms a melt pool slightly wider than the laser’s focus diameter or spot size. To achieve a solid layer, a handful of parameters, including laser power, scan speed, layer thickness, and hatch spacing, must align to produce stable melt pools. On a more macroscopic scale, nearly every L-PBF process begins with the deposition of a very thin layer of powder, on the order of tens of micrometers in thickness, onto a very flat build plate. One way the powder can be spread across the build plate is with a powder feedstock tank that is indexed up. Within the machine, the powder feedstock is kept in a receptacle whose depth is controlled by a highly precise piston. This piston moves the feedstock up in the Z direction to control the amount of powder to be deposited on a build plate. As the feedstock piston moves powder up, an analogous piston moves the build platform down a very precise distance. A recoater blade or powder scraper then pushes the powder from the feedstock platform and deposits it across the build platform. Another method for delivering a thin layer of powder is to use a gravity-fed or vacuum-fed system to dose a consistent amount of powder into a recoater blade. A powder feed mechanism directly supplied from
