 will require considerable post-processing. This is covered in detail in Chapter 5. From depowdering (or powder removal) and support removal to stress relief and hot isostatic pressing, very rarely can a part be taken from a machine and used as is. Various processes are required to ensure that the resulting part is geometrically accurate and fully dense and meets expected mechanical properties. Unlike DED, L-PBF cannot be used to build upon or repair already existing components, as it begins printing on a flat substrate bolted to the build platform. The nature of L-PBF also limits the process to a single alloy in a given part, unlike DED. If different metals are used with L-PBF within the same machine for subsequent builds, there is considerable risk of feedstock contamination.
2.2.2. PROCESS FLOW
The process flow for L-PBF can be considered to have three main sections: the digital phase, the manufacturing phase, and the post-processing phase (see Fig. 2.12) [56]. Model generation and model preparation occur in the digital
