2.2.2.1. DIGITAL PHASE
The first step of any modern manufacturing process is the design and analysis of the part using software. The desired parametric CAD file is then converted into Standard Tessellation Language, or an .stl file, which is a holdover from early stereolithography (SLA) plastic printers [56]. The .stl file is loaded into preprocessing software (e.g., Materialize Magics, Autodesk Netfab, etc.), where the part(s) can be repaired, oriented, or positioned on a virtual build plate; support structures and lattice structures can be added; and build simulations can be performed. There is a trade-off between the size of the .stl file and the resolution of the file. Next, the build is translated into a file format specific for the AM machine that was chosen to manufacture the part. Each machine vendor has its own unique file type. The build file is sliced vertically into two-dimensional cross sections, with layer thicknesses varying between 20 and 60 mm. The scan strategy (spatial pattern of the laser beams) is assigned to trace out these cross-sectional projections. Build files are then loaded into the machine and assigned build parameters (e.g., laser power, scan speed, and hatch spacing) specific to the metal and machine.
2.2.2.2. MANUFACTURING PHASE
Part production begins with machine preparation. The AM machine build platform is cleaned of residual powder with an appropriate metal powder vacuum, taking particular care to clear open bolt holes so that residual powder does not become compacted in them over time. Next, a build plate is installed on the build platform, normally with a series of bolts. Build plates are cleaned with isopropyl alcohol and dried prior to installation because a contaminated build plate (e.g., by residual machining fluid) can contaminate a substantial quantity of the powder. Care must be taken to ensure that the build plate is sufficiently flat;
