taken into account. It does, however, give AM practitioners the means to quickly compare the relative energy required to melt an alloy and a concrete starting point by which parameter sets can be translated between machines. Scan speed is the rate at which the laser moves across the build cross section, melting powder. Hatch spacing refers to the distance between adjacent laser passes, measured between the centers of the melt pools. Typically, melt-pool overlap is approximately 30%, but the exact hatch spacing is dependent on the width of the specific melt pool, which is itself alloy- and parameter-specific. Layer thickness refers to the thickness of powder feedstock deposited in each consecutive layer. This metric is directly controlled by the downward movement of the build axis. If the layer thickness is to be 20 mm, the build plate moves downward by 20 mm, and powder is deposited to fill that space (unlike in DED, where layer thickness is determined by a variety of other factors). It is worth noting that there is no single correct parameter set for a given alloy. Several parameter sets may exist that deliver satisfactory part density and mechanical properties, but may have varying responses to heat treatments. As a general rule, low laser power and low scan speeds produce more stable melt pools, which translate into more robust parameter sets that are less prone to defects and failures [57]. Oftentimes, however, it is considered acceptable to reduce build times by increasing scan speed, increasing power, or increasing layer thickness. The risk–benefit trade-offs are at the discretion of the AM engineer and the end use customer. Additional consideration may need to be given to the impacts of process parameters on part microstructure.
2.2.3.2. SCAN STRATEGY
Scan pattern refers to the pattern by which the laser traces across the part. This is not strictly a parameter; however, it does have an impact on the distribution of thermal stresses and the overall required scan time. These patterns can include continuous, stripe (or islands), and chessboard scan strategies, as illustrated in Fig. 2.13. Continuous scanning is the simplest scan strategy, whereby the laser scans back and forth across the part’s cross section. The simplicity of this scan strategy means that it is easy to implement, but it may come with other drawbacks. A stripe scan strategy rasters the laser back and forth, one stripe at a time, until the cross section is fully melted. This scan strategy results in a shorter build time than the continuous strategy and is the most widely implemented of the three strategies. Chessboard scanning selectively melts one randomized square of material at a time until the cross section is fully melted. This strategy distributes process heating and thus reduces temperature gradients across the build plane. This is particularly useful in metals that are sensitive to build distortion due to thermal stresses and is often used to reduce residual stresses in a part [58]. One downside to note, however, is that chessboard scanning does run the risk of introducing additional porosity into the part if laser timings and offsets are not precisely tuned.
