The terminating edges of the core melt pools result in an undulating surface finish that can be detrimental to final part properties, particularly in terms of fatigue performance [60]. Figure 2.15a depicts the edges of the melt pools on a hypothetical part. Figure 2.15b demonstrates this effect in a Ti6Al4V sample component. One method of smoothing these surface undulations is to assign one or two laser vectors to remelt the tips of the core laser scans. Figure 2.16 illustrates this effect both conceptually and in an example Ti6Al4V part. These are called contour passes and are vital to providing an optimized surface finish and improving low-cycle fatigue strength [61]. Contour pass parameters include laser power and scan speed; however, hatch spacing is omitted in place of contour offset, which refers to the distance that the contour centerline is offset from the CAD line. It should be noted that the means of calculating this distance varies with machine manufacturer. There is some debate within the AM community as to whether contour passes are the best means of smoothing surface roughness, but at this time, this is still considered the standard technique.
