The ultimate goal of a robust parameter development process is to identify a parameter set that produces high relative density (.99.95% RD), with small, finely (randomly dispersed) pores and favorable mechanical properties. Density measurements can be made by optical microscopy, the Archimedes method, or helium pycnometry. The Archimedes method has the benefit of being considerably faster than optical microscopy, as the cutting, mounting, and polishing steps required of optical microscopy can be completely avoided. It does not, however, provide any indication of the pore size, shape, and distribution, which are arguably more important a measure of parameter health than density measurements alone. The size and shape of the porosity in witness samples can provide insight into the health of a parameter set and powder feedstock. Although porous defects, or voids (Chapter 4), can manifest from various causes, they can be visually observed as angular and spherical. Angular or jagged porosity in an otherwise weldable alloy is usually an indication that the volumetric energy density applied to the samples should be increased. A sure indicator that a parameter set is lacking in the volumetric energy density is unmelted powder clinging to the interior surface of a pore, as can be seen in the two leftmost images in Fig. 2.17. Such voids are often referred to as LOF defects and can be remedied by increasing the laser power or decreasing the scan speed or hatch spacing [62, 63]. Spherical voids may have a range of causes, but they are typically due to gas formation in the melt pool followed by solidification. Another form of voids observed is keyhole porosity, which is angular and results from excessive energy input or volumetric energy density. This can be remedied by reducing the laser power or increasing the laser scan speed [52, 56, 64, 65].
